statement:
Take the axis-aligned rectangle whose lower-left corner is `(0, 0)` and whose upper-right corner is `(xCorner, yCorner)`. Each `circles[i] = [x_i, y_i, r_i]` is a disc centred at `(x_i, y_i)` with radius `r_i`. Discs may overlap one another and may stick out of the rectangle; a centre can even lie outside it.

Decide whether some continuous curve can run from `(0, 0)` to `(xCorner, yCorner)` such that:

- every point of it lies within the rectangle;
- it meets the rectangle's border only at its two endpoints;
- none of its points is inside a disc or on a disc's boundary circle.

The answer is `true` exactly when such a curve can be drawn, and `false` otherwise.

validator:
function validate([xCorner, yCorner, circles]) {
  const LIMIT = 1000000000;
  if (!Number.isInteger(xCorner) || !Number.isInteger(yCorner) || xCorner < 3 || yCorner < 3 || xCorner > LIMIT || yCorner > LIMIT) return 'xCorner and yCorner are integers from 3 to 10^9.';
  if (!Array.isArray(circles) || circles.length < 1 || circles.length > 1000) return 'circles needs 1 to 1000 circles.';
  for (const circle of circles) {
    if (!Array.isArray(circle) || circle.length !== 3) return 'Each circle is [x, y, r].';
    if (circle.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > LIMIT)) return 'Each x, y and r is an integer from 1 to 10^9.';
  }
  return null;
}

